-- Passage rg12-theme-summary-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('7aaa60c9-4dd2-53dd-94f3-8c7341e558e5', 'rg12-theme-summary-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Last Week at the Wash House$q$, $q$The Sunrise Wash House on Delmore Avenue was closing at the end of the month, and Esperanza Calderon had a camera from her photography class and an assignment to document "a place in transition." She chose the laundromat because she had folded towels there every Sunday of her childhood, and because its long rows of machines, humming lights, and cracked orange chairs seemed like a perfect picture of something ending.

For the first two days she photographed only objects. She shot the out-of-order sign taped to dryer twelve, the faded price chart, and the change machine with its worn buttons. The pictures were sharp and well composed, and they looked, she thought, deeply sad.

On the third day the owner, Mr. Babatunde Adeyemi, watched her photograph an empty plastic basket for nearly ten minutes. Then he walked over and asked if he could make a suggestion. "You are taking pictures of the building," he said. "The building is the part that is leaving. Take pictures of the part that stays."

Esperanza did not understand until she lowered the camera and looked around. A man was teaching his daughter to fold fitted sheets, both of them laughing at how badly it was going. Two women who had met at these machines years earlier were trading phone numbers so their Saturday conversations could continue somewhere else. Mr. Adeyemi himself was writing the address of a laundromat across town on index cards for his regular customers, along with the name of the attendant there, whom he had called to introduce them.

She spent the rest of the week photographing people. Her final project hung in the school hallway in May. It began with one image of the empty basket and ended with a picture of the index cards spread across the counter, each in Mr. Adeyemi's careful handwriting. Under the title she added a short caption: "The machines were sold. Everything else moved."$q$, 'draft')
